<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Three routes</div>
		<h1>Installation</h1>
		<p class="lede">postmarketOS is Alpine-based, so this is <b>apk</b>, not apt.
		Which route you take depends on how much you want to keep.</p>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>Read this before the first reboot.</b>
			The kernel comes with <code>base</code>. On the surya a kernel that does not
			boot is not a failed install, it is a reboot loop: the watchdog fires
			20&nbsp;s after PID&nbsp;1 and there is no screen left to fix it from. And the
			<code>boot</code> partition holds U-Boot, not the kernel &mdash; writing a
			kernel there leaves the phone unable to boot at all.
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/blob/main/flash/surya.md">flash/surya.md</a>
			has the way back in.
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>On an existing install</h2></div>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON THE PHONE</span></div>
			<pre>sudo wget -qO /etc/apk/keys/@KEY@ @URL@/keys/@KEY@
echo @URL@/main | sudo tee -a /etc/apk/repositories
sudo apk update
sudo apk add utsugi-surya-base
<b># reboot as a separate, deliberate step</b></pre>
		</div>
		<h3>What that installs</h3>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th class="mono">Package</th><th>What it brings</th></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-base</td><td><b>The phone.</b> Patched kernel,
			audio, sensors, GPS, cameras, power-off, charge mode, black box, the Plasma
			Mobile fixes and the six own applications. Nothing here is a preference: all of
			it is something that did not work.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-extras</td><td>Applications anybody could pick
			for themselves: browser, mail, two-factor, social, photos. A choice, not a fix,
			which is why it is separate &mdash; and why each one can be installed on its
			own.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-vayu-base</td><td>The experimental POCO X3 Pro set.
			The modem does not work.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p style="margin-top:20px"><a href="packages.html">Every package described, one by
		one &rarr;</a></p>

		<p style="margin-top:20px">From then on <code>apk upgrade</code> keeps everything
		current, kernel included.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>Flash a ready image</h2></div>
		<p>On the <a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases">releases
		page</a>, with everything already inside. It carries the repository line and the
		signing key, so updates are tracked from the first boot.</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON THE PC</span></div>
			<pre>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
xz -d utsugi-surya-&lt;tag&gt;.img.xz
fastboot flash userdata utsugi-surya-&lt;tag&gt;.img   <b># userdata. never boot.</b>
fastboot reboot</pre>
		</div>
		<p>On the surya, U-Boot lives in <code>boot</code> and the system lives inside a
		disk image written to <code>userdata</code>. Flashing a kernel to <code>boot</code>
		is how you lose the ability to boot at all.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>03</span><h2>Encrypted &mdash; you build it</h2></div>
		<p>There is no encrypted image to download, and that is deliberate rather than
		lazy. A LUKS master key is created when the disk is formatted, which for an image
		means on the machine that builds it. Changing the passphrase afterwards replaces
		the slot protecting that key, <b>not the key itself</b>. An encrypted image handed
		out would therefore give whoever built it the master key of every phone flashed
		from it, and no passphrase change would take it back.</p>
		<div class="sh">
			<div class="bar"><i></i><span>ON YOUR PC</span></div>
			<pre>git clone https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports
cd utsugi-pmaports
scripts/setup-workdir
scripts/build-your-own-image --encrypted</pre>
		</div>
		<p>pmbootstrap asks <b>you</b> for the passphrase during the install, and the
		master key is generated on your machine. Tested end to end on the surya: the
		initramfs opens a LUKS that lives inside a loop file on <code>userdata</code>, and
		<code>unl0kr</code> draws its keyboard on this panel.</p>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>What encryption costs here, measured.</b>
			With the root inside dm-crypt, a kernel workqueue that jams takes the whole
			filesystem with it instead of one subsystem. On 2026-09-10 an ADSP crash whose
			recovery deadlocked left this phone frozen for seven hours: load 114 with one
			task running, 96% full I/O stall, and a black screen. The cause is fixed, and
			<code>utsugi-surya-blackbox</code> now ships a watchdog that turns that state
			into a reboot with the kernel stacks saved in <code>/sys/fs/pstore</code>
			instead of a phone that sits dead until someone holds the power button.
		</div></div>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>Encrypting an existing install is not offered.</b>
			It needs the filesystem shrunk to make room for the header, it cannot run on
			the mounted root of the running system &mdash; which here lives inside a loop
			file &mdash; and it ends with the initramfs, <code>fstab</code> and
			<code>crypttab</code> rewritten by hand. Any of those half done leaves a phone
			that does not boot, and recovery here means reinstalling, which wipes. Back up,
			reinstall encrypted, restore.
		</div></div>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="devices.html"><b>Check first</b><span>What does not work</span></a>
			<a href="contribute.html"><b>Next</b><span>Contribute</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
